# Zod Validation

Runtime type validation with Zod for external data sources, API responses, and user inputs.

Apply when: validating API responses, parsing user input, handling form data, or working with external data sources.

---

## Core Principle

Type assertions (`as`) only provide compile-time safety. External data requires runtime validation.

| Approach           | Compile-time | Runtime | Use Case                    |
|--------------------|--------------|---------|------------------------------|
| Type assertion     | ✓            | ✗       | Never for external data      |
| Zod validation     | ✓            | ✓       | API responses, user input    |

---

## Why Zod

Zod is widely adopted, has excellent TypeScript inference, and provides a clean API. Alternatives include Valibot (smaller bundle) and Arktype (TypeScript-like syntax). TypeScript types are erased at compile time, so runtime validation always requires a separate schema definition regardless of library choice.

---

## Conventions

### Naming

| Entity         | Convention              | Example                      |
|----------------|-------------------------|------------------------------|
| Schema         | `PascalCase` + `Schema` | `UserSchema`, `ConfigSchema` |
| Inferred type  | `PascalCase`            | `type User = z.infer<...>`   |
| Schema file    | Matches domain          | `schemas/user.ts`            |

### When to Validate

| Boundary                | Validate? | Rationale                          |
|-------------------------|-----------|------------------------------------|
| API responses           | Always    | External data is never trusted     |
| User/form input         | Always    | Untrusted by definition            |
| Environment variables   | Always    | Validate at startup, fail fast     |
| Function arguments      | No        | TypeScript handles internal calls  |
| Database query results  | No        | Schema migrations handle integrity |

### Parse vs SafeParse

| Context                          | Method         | Rationale                              |
|----------------------------------|----------------|----------------------------------------|
| Request handlers / API routes    | `.safeParse()` | Return structured errors to callers    |
| App startup (env, config)        | `.parse()`     | Fail fast, crash is appropriate        |
| Data pipelines / batch jobs      | `.safeParse()` | Log and skip bad records               |

---

## API Response Validation

```typescript
// Bad - No runtime validation
const fetchUser = async (id: string): Promise<User> => {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const data = await response.json();
  return data as User; // DANGEROUS
};

// Good - Check HTTP status + Zod validation
const fetchUser = async (id: string): Promise<User> => {
  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
  }
  const data = await response.json();
  return UserSchema.parse(data);
};
```

---

## Error Handling Strategy

Use `.safeParse()` in request handlers to return structured errors. Never expose raw Zod errors to clients.

```typescript
const result = RequestSchema.safeParse(body);
if (!result.success) {
  // Log full error internally
  logger.warn('Validation failed', { error: result.error.format() });
  // Return safe summary to client
  return Response.json(
    { error: 'Invalid request', fields: result.error.flatten().fieldErrors },
    { status: 400 },
  );
}
```

---

## Schema as Single Source of Truth

Derive types from schemas — never define both independently. This eliminates drift between runtime validation and compile-time types.

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const UserSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().uuid(),
  name: z.string().min(1),
  email: z.string().email(),
});

// Derive — never manually duplicate this type
type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;
```

---

## Organization

- Co-locate schema with the code that uses it by default
- Extract to `schemas/` only when shared across multiple modules
- Keep schema and inferred type in the same file

```
src/
├── schemas/
│   ├── user.ts      # UserSchema + User type (shared)
│   └── api.ts       # API response schemas (shared)
├── services/
│   └── user.ts      # Uses UserSchema
├── routes/
│   └── users.ts     # Defines RequestSchema inline (local only)
```

---

## Quick Reference

| Task                    | Convention                                              |
|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| Name a schema           | `PascalCaseSchema` (e.g., `UserSchema`)                 |
| Derive a type           | `type T = z.infer<typeof TSchema>` — never duplicate    |
| Validate API responses  | Always — `.parse()` or `.safeParse()`                   |
| Validate internal calls | Don't — rely on TypeScript                              |
| Handle errors in routes | `.safeParse()` + structured 400 response                |
| Validate at startup     | `.parse()` — fail fast on bad config                    |
| Expose Zod errors       | Never to clients — log internally, return safe summary  |

---

## Checklist

- [ ] No type assertions (`as`) for external data
- [ ] HTTP response status checked before parsing (`response.ok`)
- [ ] Zod schemas for all API responses and user inputs
- [ ] Types derived from schemas — not manually duplicated
- [ ] `.safeParse()` in request handlers with structured error responses
- [ ] `.parse()` for startup/config validation (fail fast)
- [ ] Raw Zod errors never exposed to clients
- [ ] Schema naming follows `PascalCaseSchema` convention